WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.387
Abstaining on purpose.

2
00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:07.597
“I don’t know based on the provided sources” is a correct answer whenever the sources don’t contain one.

3
00:00:07.877 --> 00:00:19.803
Anthropic’s first recommendation for reducing hallucinations is to give the model explicit permission to admit uncertainty, and in RAG you can go further, because you have signals the model doesn’t.

4
00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:22.045
Design abstention in three layers.

5
00:00:22.325 --> 00:00:29.024
In the prompt, say exactly when to abstain and give one exact sentence, so code can detect it without guessing.

6
00:00:29.304 --> 00:00:41.613
Before generation, add a cheap gate: if the best retrieval or reranker score is below a threshold you calibrated on your eval set, skip the model call and abstain, or ask a clarifying question.

7
00:00:41.893 --> 00:00:48.144
That saves money on questions you can’t answer and removes the temptation for the model to improvise.

8
00:00:48.424 --> 00:01:01.651
After generation, treat an answer whose substantive claims have no verified citations as an abstention in disguise: show the sources you found and say they don’t settle it, rather than shipping unsupported text.

9
00:01:01.931 --> 00:01:05.664
Then measure it, in two directions, because each can be gamed alone.

10
00:01:05.944 --> 00:01:10.979
Abstention recall is the share of unanswerable questions where the system abstained.

11
00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:16.229
False abstention rate is the share of answerable questions where it abstained anyway.

12
00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:20.776
A system that always says “I don’t know” has perfect recall and is useless.

13
00:01:21.056 --> 00:01:28.139
Your eval set needs both kinds of question for these numbers to exist, which is one of the requirements in lesson 12.
